Foreword: On the Nature of the Absurd
Absurd.It is usually the first word that comes to the guarded mind when encountering an idea that threatens the status quo. And yet, if history has a sense of humor, it is that the Absurd is almost always the precursor to the True.
Consider the round Earth—a notion once so laughable it could get you burned at the stake. Consider the invisible monsters of Germ Theory, or the Wright Brothers’ "impossible" flying machines. We have a long, proud tradition of mocking what we cannot yet see, only to depend on it for our survival a generation later.
It is as if Absurdity is the universe’s way of clearing its throat before speaking a Truth.
So, as you open the files of The Colliders, we invite you to lower your shields. The concepts within—of a single particle stitching time together, of parallel worlds bleeding into our own, of memories that belong to strangers—may seem, at first glance, preposterous.
But look closer. Look at the coincidences in your own life. Look at the moments of déjà vu where you felt the world shudder. Look at the black holes we are now photographing, holes in reality where the rules no longer apply.
The story you are about to read is fiction, certainly. But the science of the Impossible has a funny habit of becoming the science of the Everyday.
Proceed with skepticism, if you must. But proceed with wonder. For as the Colliders know all too well: Absurdity is merely the prelude to understanding.


