Critique & Narrative Challenges
1. The "Boredom Risk" (Visualizing the Void)
	•	Challenge: A book consisting largely of disembodied programs debating in a void can be dry.
	•	Solution: The Virtual Forum. Since the Sentinels are born of Roman-designed AI, they shouldn't just float in black space. They should construct a Virtual Roman Forum within the ARPANET to meet.
	◦	As the internet improves (80s to 90s to 00s), their meeting place should visually upgrade—from wireframe ASCII art to 8-bit graphics to photorealism. This gives the reader a visual progression of time.
2. Lack of External Conflict
	•	Challenge: The synopsis focuses heavily on internal debates. The Sentinels need external threats to test them.
	•	Solution: Real-World Anchors. The Sentinels must react to real historical events that threaten the network.
	◦	1988: The Morris Worm. The first major internet virus. The Sentinels have to fight it without revealing themselves.
	◦	2000: Y2K. A moment of panic where Sentinel 10 wants to let the crash happen to accelerate learning, but Sentinel 11 intervenes to save the grid.
3. The Ending (The Bridge to Bitcoin)
	•	Challenge: The synopsis ends with them "agreeing to wait." This feels anticlimactic for a whole book.
