The Colliders Saga: Master Synopsis
Logline: A single electron weaves the fabric of time. When a Roman shipwreck fractures reality into two timeline strands, a group of digital survivors from a lost utopia must manipulate the history of the 20th century to resurrect their world—using a lonely 1980s scientist and an ancient AI named Satoshi to do it.

Book 0: The Architecture of Eternity
The Philosophical Foundation
	•	Foreword: "Absurd" – A disarming introduction that invites the reader to suspend disbelief, positing that the "Absurd" (Round Earth, Germ Theory, Time Travel) is merely Truth waiting for a generation brave enough to accept it.
	•	Part I: The Single Electron: Based on the Feynman-Wheeler concept, this narrative establishes that there is only one electron in the universe, bouncing backward and forward through time to create the illusion of matter.
	◦	The Knot: Black Holes are revealed as the "turning points" where the electron switches direction.
	◦	Déjà Vu: Defined not as a brain glitch, but as a momentary collision where the electron crosses its own path—a "short circuit" in the weave of reality.
	◦	The Consciousness: Over eons of solitude, this single particle gains awareness. It yearns to know itself.
	•	Part II: The Breath Before Time: A mythic account of the Big Bang described as a single inhalation. It sets the stakes: our entire universe is just one breath in an infinite cycle.

THE ANCIENT ARC (The Fracture)
Book 1: The Book of Lucius (67 BC)
The Scholar and the Tragedy
	•	Setting: Rome, The Mediterranean.
	•	The Protagonists: Lucius Fabius (a pacifist scholar), his brother Marcus (a stoic soldier), and their apprentice Marcellus (the young, arrogant Mark Antony).
	•	The Mission: They are tasked with transporting a Monazite Urn (containing heavy, radioactive isotopes used for "temple magic") and the Antikythera Mechanism (The Celestial Computer) back to Rome.
	•	The Dynamic: Lucius believes the Mechanism will bring enlightenment; Marcus believes it is a weapon; Marcellus believes it is a path to power.
	•	The Climax: A massive storm strikes the ship The Aurelia.
Book 3: The Book of Mark (67 BC)
The Mirror and the Split
	•	The Incident: This book zooms in on the specific millisecond of the timeline fracture.
	•	The Mirror: In the chaos of the storm, young Marcellus holds a polished Bronze Mirror near the Monazite Urn. He catches his own reflection and experiences a violent moment of Déjà Vu (the timeline trembling).
	•	The Choice: In a fit of vanity and panic, he pushes the mirror away.
	•	The Event: Lightning strikes the deck. It arcs through the Monazite Urn (Fuel), melts the Bronze Mirror (Reflector), and fuses them with the brass gears of the Mechanism (Conductor).
	•	The Creation: The result is The Shard—a lump of Fused Anomalous Matter. Inside it, a single particle from the mirror becomes quantumly entangled with its twin in the alternate timeline where the ship survived.
	•	The Divergence:
	◦	Universe A (Ours): The ship burns and sinks. The Mechanism is lost. The Dark Ages eventually follow. The Shard sinks to the coral reef.
	◦	Universe B (The Omni): The ship survives. Rome creates a technological singularity. A 2,000-year Utopia follows.
Book 2: The Book of Marcus (67 BC)
The Soldier and the Heist
	•	Perspective: Told from Marcus’s POV. Before the voyage, he leads a daring heist to retrieve the Mechanism from pirates (The "Celestial Cartographer" arc).
	•	The Ritual: Marcus participates in a Mithraic ritual where he hallucinates a "Ketos" (Sea Monster). He is unknowingly tapping into the quantum resonance of the future Sentinels.
	•	The Aftermath: In Universe A, Marcus survives the wreck on "Crocodile Rock" but believes the mission failed. He returns to Rome a hardened man. In Universe B (implied), he becomes the General of the Omni, securing the peace that allows the computer age to begin in 50 BC.

THE BRIDGE (The Haunting)
Book 3.5: The Book of Eleni (1901)
The First Victim
	•	Setting: Antikythera / Symi, 1901.
	•	The Discovery: Eleni, a female sponge diver, retrieves the Shard and a vial of "Black Dust" (the Monazite fuel) from the wreck.
