I. The Foreword (Book 0)

Title: On the Nature of the Absurd
Absurd.
It is usually the first word that comes to the guarded mind when encountering an idea that threatens the status quo. It is a defensive word, a shield we raise against the impossible. And yet, if history has a sense of humor, it is that the Absurd is almost always the precursor to the True.
Consider the round Earth—a notion once so laughable it could get you burned at the stake. Consider the invisible monsters of Germ Theory, which were mocked by the finest doctors of the age until they washed their hands and stopped killing their patients. Consider the Wright Brothers’ "impossible" flying machines, dismissed by physicists weeks before they took flight.
We have a long, proud tradition of mocking what we cannot yet see, only to depend on it for our survival a generation later. It is as if Absurdity is the universe’s way of clearing its throat before speaking a Truth.
So, as you open the files of The Colliders, I invite you to lower your shields.
The concepts within—of a single particle stitching time together, of parallel worlds bleeding into our own, of memories that belong to strangers—may seem, at first glance, preposterous. But look closer. Look at the coincidences in your own life. Look at the moments of déjà vu where you felt the world shudder. Look at the black holes we are now photographing, literal punctures in reality where the rules no longer apply.
The story you are about to read is fiction, certainly. But the science of the Impossible has a funny habit of becoming the science of the Everyday.
Proceed with skepticism, if you must. But proceed with wonder. For as the Colliders know all too well: Absurdity is merely the prelude to understanding.


Chapter 4.5: Echo Beach

August 16, 1982 – Kythera
The cove was a geological amphitheater, a perfect crescent of white sand cupped by limestone cliffs that looked like hardened wax.
“Jackpot,” Mikey grinned, abandoning his trainers in the sand. He and Liz headed straight for the water, their voices bright and loud, bouncing off the cliff walls like ricochets.
Elena Petrakis lingered near the cliff face, some thirty feet away, examining the fossilized strata. “It’s remarkable,” she called out, her voice carrying clearly in the stillness. “The acoustics here… it’s like a cathedral.”
James wandered away from them, drawn toward a specific formation near the center of the cove: a flat, weathered slab of limestone that jutted out of the sand like a natural altar. It sat in the dead center of the cliff’s parabolic curve.
He sat down. He unslung his canvas daypack, placing it on the rock next to him. The Shard, buried deep inside, seemed to radiate a cold weight through the fabric.
James looked out at the impossible turquoise of the Aegean. He felt a sudden, overwhelming sense of peace, a feeling that this specific spot existed outside of time.
“It’s… perfect,” James whispered.
The words left his lips, but they didn’t fade. He heard his own voice, “It’s perfect,” hang in the air, suspended with a metallic reverb.
Then, the air pressure dropped. The hairs on his arms stood up.
From his immediate left—right where his bag sat—a woman’s voice gasped. It was breathless, terrified, and sounded as if she were hiding in a small, enclosed space, huddled right against his shoulder.
“Ποιος είναι εκεί;” (Who is there?)
James froze. Before he could process the Greek, a third voice cut through the static. It came from the exact same spot, layering over the Greek woman’s fear. It was a voice he knew intimately—Elena’s voice—but it was strained, cracked with panic, and sounded wrong.
“Don’t let anyone else touch it!”
James scrambled backward off the rock, his heels digging into the soft sand. He nearly tripped over his own feet, heart hammering against his ribs.
He spun around. Elena was standing exactly where she had been a moment ago, examining a rock face thirty feet away. She was calm. She was silent.
“Elena?” James shouted, his voice cracking.
Elena turned, startled by his tone. She began walking toward him, shading her eyes. “James? What is it?”
James stood his ground, pointing a shaking finger at the rock he had just vacated. “Why did you say that?”
Elena stopped, a frown creasing her brow. “Say what?”
“You shouted at me!” James advanced on her, the adrenaline making his movements jerky. “Just now. You whispered in Greek, and then you shouted, ‘Don’t let anyone else touch it.’”
Elena’s face fell into a mask of pure confusion. She looked toward the water where Liz and Mikey were splashing, then back at James. “James… I didn’t say a word. I was looking at the limestone fossils.”
“I heard you!” James insisted, stepping closer, his eyes searching hers. “You were right next to me! I heard you as clearly as I hear you now.”
“James, look at me,” Elena said softly, reaching out to touch his arm. Her skin was warm; the voice he had heard had been cold. “I am standing right here. I didn’t shout. I didn’t whisper. I haven’t moved from the cliff.”
“But it was your voice,” James pressed, desperate for her to admit it was a prank. “It was you.”
“It wasn’t,” she said firmly.
James stared at her. He replayed the sound in his head, the memory of it echoing in his mind. He realized with a sickening jolt that the voice hadn’t sounded like the Elena standing in front of him—the 24-year-old grad student in a sundress with sun-warmed skin.
“No…” James murmured, backing down slightly. “No, you’re right. It… it sounded like you, but…” He rubbed his face, trying to find the words. “It wobbled. It sounded terrified. It sounded… older. Like you’d been screaming for a long time.”
Elena looked at him with deep concern. “James, it’s the heat. You’ve been in the sun all morning.”
“Whatever, Astro-boy,” a booming voice cut in.
Mikey was wading up the beach, shaking water from his hair like a wet dog, Liz trailing behind him. He looked between the two of them, sensing the tension but deciding to smash through it.
“What’s the drama? James having a profound realization about his lack of tanning ability?”
“He thinks he heard voices,” Elena said, trying to keep it light but watching James carefully. “He thought I yelled at him not to touch something.”
“Don’t touch it?” Mikey scoffed, grabbing his towel. He looked up at the towering, concave cliffs surrounding them. “Mate, look at the shape of this place. It’s a giant satellite dish. You probably heard a goat halfway up the mountain having a bleat, and the limestone bounced it right into your ear.”
James shook his head. “It was words, Mikey. It wasn’t a goat.”
“You’d be surprised,” Mikey grinned, seizing the opportunity. “Greek goats are very articulate. Very tragic creatures. Listen…”
Mikey cleared his throat, puffed out his chest, and threw his voice toward the cliffs with a theatrical vibrato.
“Jaaaaaames! Doooooon’t toooooouch iiiiiiit!”
He ended the impression with a convulsing bleat that echoed absurdly around the cove.
Liz snorted, covering her mouth. Even Elena cracked a small, reluctant smile.
“See?” Mikey said, slapping James on the shoulder with a wet hand. “Just a goat with a speech impediment, mate. Or maybe the Oracle of Delphi telling you to lay off the retsina. Come on, let’s pack up. The Minotaur awaits, and I’m starving.”
The tension in the air shattered. The supernatural dread was dissolved by the sheer stupidity of the moment. Mikey and Liz began gathering their things, chatting loudly about whether the taverna in the village served chips.
James stood still for a moment longer. He looked back at the flat limestone slab. It sat innocuous and silent in the sun.
He walked over to it slowly. He grabbed his daypack by the strap, flinching slightly as he did so, half-expecting the voice to return. It didn’t. He dragged the bag off the rock, careful not to let it bump against his leg.

Twenty minutes later, the blue Fiat was rattling back up the dirt track, dust billowing in its wake.
The mood in the car was high. Mikey was wrestling with the cassette deck, blasting Duran Duran, singing along loudly and out of key. Liz was hanging her arm out the window, letting the wind catch her hand, laughing at something Elena had said.
James sat in the passenger seat, staring out at the passing olive groves. His hand rested on the canvas bag at his feet.
He touched the velvet pouch through the fabric. It was still cold.
He looked at Elena in the rearview mirror. She was laughing, her head thrown back, the sun catching her dark hair. She looked young. She looked happy.
He tried to force himself to join in, to believe Mikey’s joke. Just a goat. Just the wind.
But the memory of that voice—her voice, cracked with a grief he hadn't yet caused—stayed with him, vibrating in his ears deeper than the synth-pop bass, whispering a warning he knew he had already failed to heed.


