ng with me. Maybe it’ll bring me luck with the bosons.”Packing for Geneva was a whirlwind. Amidst textbooks, sensible jumpers, and his treasured record collection (Joy Division, The Smiths, a bit of Kraftwerk for good measure), the Shard was carefully wrapped in its velvet bag and placed in a side pocket of his sturdy leather briefcase. It wasn't a scientific priority for his CERN work, not officially. He wouldn't be running experiments on it. But it was too intriguing, too deeply entwined with the memory of that sun-drenched Greek summer and his budding connection with Elena, to leave behind. It felt like a part of his journey now, a small piece of ancient mystery that would accompany him to the cutting edge of modern science.

Chapter 7: Frontiers of Physics
Geneva, January 1983. The air was crisp, alpine-cold, a world away from the gentle damp of Cambridge. CERN was a sprawling complex, a city dedicated to science, its buildings a functional, almost stark contrast to the picturesque Swiss landscape. James, wrapped in his warmest coat, felt a potent mix of intimidation and sheer, unadulterated exhilaration. He was here. This was it.
